  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe improved approach combines multi-resolution optimization with advanced gradient-based methods to escape local minima and refine solutions effectively.\n\nKey innovations:\n- Multi-resolution optimization: Start with a coarse grid, optimize, then interpolate to finer grids for refinement, allowing efficient exploration followed by precision.\n- Adam optimizer with adaptive learning rates: Faster convergence and escape from local minima compared to basic gradient descent with fixed learning rates.\n- Better constraint handling: Use projection steps after each update to maintain feasibility, ensuring 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and sum constraint.\n- Dynamic resolution scaling: Balance between computational cost and precision by starting coarse and switching to fine only when justified.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nObjective: Minimize C5 = max_k [\u222b h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx] over shifts k in [-2,2]\nSubject to:\n- 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n- \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nExact representation:\n- Discretized as h \u2208 \u211d^{n_points}, h[i] \u2208 [0,1], sum(h) = n_points / 2\n- Overlap integral computed as max(np.correlate(h, 1 - h, mode='full') * dx)\n\nNew elements:\n- Coarse-fine grid transfer using linear interpolation\n- Adam optimization with adaptive learning rates\n- Projection operation to maintain constraints\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Initialization:\n1. Use initial_h_values if provided, else start with uniform h\n2. Start with coarse resolution (n_start = 50)\n3. If initial_h available, downsample to coarse grid using interpolation\n\nb) Coarse grid optimization:\n1. Apply Adam optimizer for 2000 steps or until convergence\n2. Compute gradient via centered finite differences\n3. Project to feasible region after each step\n4. Track best solution found\n\nc) Transfer to fine grid:\n1. Interpolate best coarse h to n_points = 200\n2. Normalize and clip to meet constraints\n\nd) Fine grid optimization:\n1. Continue optimization with Adam for 2000 steps\n2. More precise gradient estimation\n3. Track best solution and validate with exact evaluator\n\ne) Constraint handling:\nAfter each step:\n1. Clip h to [0,1]\n2. Rescale to ensure sum(h) = n_points / 2\n\nf) Termination:\n1. Stop if time budget exhausted or no improvement for 200 steps\n2. Return best h found along with its C5 value\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Initial grid size: 50 (coarse)\n- Fine grid size: 200 (balance between precision and cost)\n- Adam parameters: lr=0.01, \u03b21=0.9, \u03b22=0.999\n- Gradient step size: 1e-4\n- Start with default initial_h if available, else uniform\n- Early stopping: 200 steps without improvement\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Coarse optimization: 2000 steps, ~200ms each\n- Transfer: ~50ms\n- Fine optimization: 2000 steps, ~250ms each\n- Expected total: ~100s\n- Robustness: Early stopping, best solution retention, feasible fallback\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Final h validated through evaluate_erdos_solution\n- Check sum constraint with tolerance 1e-6\n- Return formatted as (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)",
